Annotations: |
  Defines Nest's platform-adapter boundary: the abstract surface every HTTP platform
  integration (Express, Fastify, and others) must implement so the rest of the
  framework (router, application bootstrap) can drive HTTP request/response handling
  without knowing which underlying HTTP library is in use.

---

"AbstractHttpAdapter(instance: any)":
  location: http-adapter.ts
  annotations: |
    Abstract base class for platform HTTP adapters. Concrete subclasses (Express,
    Fastify, and others) wrap a specific HTTP library instance behind this common
    surface. Routing-verb methods (get/post/put/...) default to delegating to
    `instance`; lifecycle and response-shaping operations are left abstract for
    subclasses to implement against their underlying library's request/response
    objects.

    `instance`: the underlying platform application instance (e.g. an Express app)

    Algorithm:
    1. Concrete subclasses call the base constructor with an already-created
       platform application object, or let the base class default-construct one
    2. setHttpServer/getHttpServer expose the raw listening server so the rest of
       the framework (e.g. graceful shutdown) can reach it directly
  properties:
    "httpServer -> any": |
      The underlying native HTTP/HTTPS server once initHttpServer has run.
  methods:
    "use(args: Array<any>) -> result: any": |
      Register platform-level middleware; forwards to the underlying instance's own
      use method.
    "getInstance() -> instance: any": |
      Return the underlying platform application instance (e.g. the Express app).
    "setHttpServer(httpServer: any)": |
      Bind the raw listening server obtained from initHttpServer.
    "normalizePath(path: string) -> path: string": |
      Convert a Nest route path into the format the underlying router expects.
      Overridden by adapters whose router has stricter path-token rules.
    "mapException(error: any) -> mapped: any": |
      Translate a platform-specific parsing error (e.g. a body-parser syntax error)
      into a framework exception before it reaches the exception-handling pipeline.
      The base implementation is the identity function.
    "initHttpServer(options: Object<string, any>)": |
      Abstract. Create the concrete HTTP/HTTPS/HTTP2 server bound to the instance
      returned by getInstance, and assign it via setHttpServer.
    "close()": |
      Abstract. Stop accepting new connections and shut down the underlying server.
    "reply(response: any, body: any, statusCode: number)": |
      Abstract. Write `body` as the HTTP response, setting `statusCode` when
      supplied.
    "status(response: any, statusCode: number)": |
      Abstract. Set the HTTP response status code.
    "setErrorHandler(handler: Object<string, any>, prefix: string)": |
      Abstract. Register the framework's central error-handling middleware,
      optionally scoped under a path `prefix`.
    "setNotFoundHandler(handler: Object<string, any>, prefix: string)": |
      Abstract. Register the framework's fallback handler for unmatched routes.
    "createMiddlewareFactory(requestMethod: string) -> factory: Object<string, any>": |
      Abstract. Build a path/callback registration function bound to a specific HTTP
      method, used when wiring per-method middleware.
    "applyVersionFilter(handler: Object<string, any>, version: string | Array<string>, versioningOptions: Object<string, any>) -> filtered: Object<string, any>": |
      Abstract. Wrap `handler` so it only runs when the incoming request matches
      `version`, per the configured versioning strategy (URI, header, media-type, or
      custom).

